        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:23:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105174,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar osseous choristomas are typically unilateral and located in the superotemporal conjunctiva {22496017; 29589231; 22516954; 13871021}. They can also occur temporally, as well as (exceptionally) in the inferotemporal conjunctiva {13871021}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:23:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105175,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients with epibulbar osseous choristoma present with a yellow, orange, pink, or white subconjunctival mass that may be multinodular and may be freely mobile or adherent to the sclera or muscles. The mass usually extends from the equator to the superotemporal fornix. Epibulbar osseous choristomas are firm, and calcification can be seen using CT {22496017}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:23:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105176,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The incidence of epibulbar osseous choristoma is unknown. Fewer than 80&nbsp;cases have been reported. They are present from birth and frequently occur in patients younger than 20&nbsp;years {22496017; 12358295}, but they can also be discovered later in life {18569837}. There appears to be a slight female predilection {13871021; 12358295}. Most reported cases have been in White patients. However, a few cases in African-American patients have been reported {22516954; 13871021}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:23:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
