  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105179,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epibulbar osseous choristoma manifests as a firm whitish-yellowish mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-13T16:23:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105180,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "<p>Epibulbar osseous choristomas are composed of compact lamellar bone with normal osteocytes. Haversian canals can be found. Haematopoietic bone marrow is usually absent but has been reported (rarely) {12358295}. The bone is surrounded by dense fibrous tissue (periosteum) and an internal osteogenic layer {13871021}. Other types of tissue than bone, such as lacrimal acini, cartilage, nerves, or adipose tissue, may be present in the context of complex choristoma {22496017; 7733194; 3681614; 12358295}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-07-19T08:17:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
